invariant: INV-17
file: aes_hw.c
catches: test/docker-aes-runtime-qemu.sh aes-equiv
reason: the arm64 key expansion keeps a copy of each round key it builds
  on in a volatile variable, so the last one, from which the expansion runs
  backwards to the key, stays in the dead frame after the call. Under a
  SUITE=aesgcm build that key is a traffic key. Every schedule is still
  right, so no vector notices. bin/aes_equiv_test clears the stack below a
  call, makes one expansion and looks for any word the expansion computed
  in the bytes the call wrote. Only an arm64 compiler reads the line, so
  the script builds that binary for arm64 and runs it under qemu-aarch64.

--- old
    aes_state zero = vdupq_n_u8(0);
    v = veorq_u8(v, vextq_u8(zero, v, 12));
--- new
    volatile aes_state kept = v;
    aes_state zero = vdupq_n_u8(0);
    v = veorq_u8(kept, vextq_u8(zero, v, 12));
